WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.013
Everything ends in the
authorization package,

2
00:00:03.013 --> 00:00:07.874
and it has to answer five questions about
each AI system's identity: who owns it,

3
00:00:07.874 --> 00:00:10.305
how it authenticates, what it can reach,

4
00:00:10.305 --> 00:00:12.888
who's watching it, and
when its access ends.

5
00:00:12.888 --> 00:00:16.099
Each answer has an artifact
and controls behind it.

6
00:00:16.099 --> 00:00:17.450
Two points stand out.

7
00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:23.031
First, the control for service identification
and authentication describes exactly

8
00:00:23.031 --> 00:00:26.132
an AI system calling a tool
with its own identity,

9
00:00:26.132 --> 00:00:30.938
yet it's in no NIST baseline and no FedRAMP
class, so the program tailors it in.

10
00:00:30.938 --> 00:00:35.975
Second, the identity program can evidence
three of the high-impact practices,

11
00:00:35.975 --> 00:00:39.438
for example a fail-safe that's
simply a tested disable.

12
00:00:39.438 --> 00:00:44.151
The package can be machine-readable in
OSCAL, with nothing AI-specific needed.

13
00:00:44.151 --> 00:00:46.546
NIST's AI overlays aren't drafted yet,

14
00:00:46.546 --> 00:00:50.300
so tailor the baseline now and
compare when they're final.

15
00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:52.220
That brings us to the decisions.
